1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,921
Pull a heart cell out,

2
00:00:01,921 --> 00:00:03,057
alone in a dish.

3
00:00:03,057 --> 00:00:04,100
It still beats.

4
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:04,679
Why?

5
00:00:04,679 --> 00:00:06,775
Every single heart muscle cell can

6
00:00:06,775 --> 00:00:09,199
generate its own heartbeat completely by

7
00:00:09,199 --> 00:00:11,189
itself, no nerve or brain signal

8
00:00:11,189 --> 00:00:11,906
needed.

9
00:00:11,906 --> 00:00:14,287
Put two differently-timed heart cells side

10
00:00:14,287 --> 00:00:14,807
by side,

11
00:00:14,807 --> 00:00:16,728
and within moments they lock into

12
00:00:16,728 --> 00:00:18,379
exactly the same rhythm.

13
00:00:18,379 --> 00:00:19,847
So is it just these tiny

14
00:00:19,847 --> 00:00:21,490
channels doing all the work?

15
00:00:21,490 --> 00:00:22,367
Not quite.

16
00:00:22,367 --> 00:00:24,832
Some researchers have synced heart cells

17
00:00:24,832 --> 00:00:27,574
using nothing but tiny mechanical vibrations,

18
00:00:27,574 --> 00:00:29,536
no physical contact at all.

19
00:00:29,536 --> 00:00:31,010
Inside your real heart,

20
00:00:31,010 --> 00:00:33,389
one region beats naturally faster than

21
00:00:33,389 --> 00:00:34,493
every other cell,

22
00:00:34,493 --> 00:00:36,424
and the rest simply follow its

23
00:00:36,424 --> 00:00:37,042
pace.

24
00:00:37,042 --> 00:00:38,649
If every single cell in your

25
00:00:38,649 --> 00:00:40,256
heart can keep its own beat,

26
00:00:40,256 --> 00:00:42,159
what do you think actually decides

27
00:00:42,159 --> 00:00:43,185
which rhythm wins?
